hello everyone so today i want to talk a  little bit about memorization but more  so about becoming familiar with  something and the different layers of  understanding that goes behind it  something that i've always struggled  with especially when learning a new  piece and i just want to relate it to a  little anecdote i have in mind  so when i first moved to new york city  for college i had a special friend who  showed me around and among one of the  first places we went of course was  central park  i remember i was pretty amazed at how  big the whole park was and i was even  more amazed at how my friend really knew  his way around  almost as if he was walking around his  own backyard  of course we hid all the main  attractions within the park  but we went to all these side trails  where i was pretty convinced we'd get  lost very quickly but he always seemed  to know his way around  he had a really good understanding of  the aerial point of view of the park as  if he was following a map in his head  as well as all of the details he knew  from walking around  he'd used key buildings you can see from  the park as a reference and he also knew  how hilly certain aries got the location  of the entrances  the different ponds certain trees and  corners bridges  benches pretty much anything about a  specific section of the park that he  absolutely adored  it was pretty cool how he knew so much  information about the park and he had a  personal connection to so many of the  different parts and characteristics of  all these different areas  so relating this to music a lot of times  i actually don't memorize something  consciously and instead i rely on just  becoming familiar with the music through  repetition  as a result my memory ends up being  inconsistent and also i don't end up  having a full understanding of the music  to relate it back to the park it's as if  i walk through without much thought and  i only have a few points of reference as  i'm navigating through  i think a better approach is to know a  piece in the way that my friend knew the  park which involves different points of  view  sectionalizing having multiple points of  reference  and a thorough understanding of the  layout basically knowing and  understanding something in multiple ways  so for example let's listen to a little  bit of scrub and sonata number four  [Music]  going back to the beginning you can look  at it from a harmonic point of view  [Music]  you can also break it down into multiple  lines different phrases  [Music]  intervallic relations  more descriptive terms  formal analysis  [Music]  and whole list of other ways to look at  a piece  so i hope that made sense i think the  main point is that the more ways we know  something the better off we are and  the more we learn the more that's going  to be possible  so thanks for watching and i'll see you  next time  [Music]
 

